1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Welcome to video five.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Now that our environment is configured,

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
it's time to install all dependencies

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
and get VentureX running.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,000
We'll walk through Composer, npm, Vite,

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,000
database migrations, and launching the dev server.

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Let's go.

8
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,000
We start with Composer.

9
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Run composer install.

10
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
This reads your composer.lock file and downloads

11
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
every PHP package the project depends on —

12
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Laravel framework, Livewire components,

13
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Tailwind CSS build tools, testing libraries, and more.

14
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,000
On a fresh install, this typically takes thirty to ninety seconds

15
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
depending on your internet connection.

16
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,000
You'll see a progress bar as packages download,

17
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
then an autoload generation step at the end.

18
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
If you see a memory limit error,

19
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,000
increase PHP's memory_limit in your php.ini file

20
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
to at least 512 megabytes.

21
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Next, install frontend dependencies with npm install.

22
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
This downloads Node packages —

23
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Tailwind CSS 4, Livewire, Alpine.js,

24
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Vite build system, and related plugins

25
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,000
into the node_modules folder.

26
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,000
First-time installs take a bit longer

27
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
as npm resolves the dependency tree.

28
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,000
You'll see a summary of packages added

29
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:53,000
and any peer dependency warnings.

30
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Warnings are typically safe to ignore

31
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,000
unless they indicate a breaking conflict.

32
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Now compile the frontend assets.

33
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Run npm run build.

34
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
This triggers Vite — our JavaScript build tool.

35
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Vite reads vite.config.js, processes your Tailwind CSS files,

36
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
compiles JavaScript modules, and outputs optimized bundles

37
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,000
to the public/build directory.

38
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
For VentureX, expect roughly 95 kilobytes of CSS

39
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
and 47 kilobytes of JavaScript after minification.

40
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:37,000
The build takes about ten to twenty seconds.

41
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Watch for the output summary — it shows each chunk,

42
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,000
its size, and any warnings about large bundles.

43
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Verify the build by checking

44
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
public/build/manifest.json.

45
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,000
This file maps source files to their compiled versions.

46
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,000
You should see entries for the main CSS and JS bundles.

47
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
The public/build/assets folder contains

48
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,000
the actual compiled files with content-hashed filenames

49
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
for cache busting.

50
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Now create the database tables and populate demo data.

51
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Run php artisan migrate --seed.

52
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
This executes every migration file

53
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
in database/migrations in chronological order.

54
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
You'll see each table being created —

55
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
users, contacts, companies, deals,

56
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,000
tasks, invoices, products, ai_insights, error_logs,

57
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,000
and dozens more.

58
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,000
VentureX has over fifty migration files

59
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
covering the full ERP and CRM schema.

60
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:57,000
After all tables are created, the seeder runs.

61
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:01,000
It inserts demo companies, contacts, deals with various stages,

62
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:05,000
sample invoices, product catalogs,

63
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,000
AI-generated insights, and two admin user accounts.

64
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Watch for the green check marks —

65
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,000
each confirms a migration ran successfully.

66
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,000
If you see a table already exists error,

67
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,000
your database may have old tables.

68
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:29,000
You can run php artisan migrate:fresh --seed

69
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
to drop everything and start over.

70
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Let's verify the database.

71
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Open your MySQL client and connect

72
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,000
to the VENTUREX_ERP database.

73
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
Run SHOW TABLES and you should see

74
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
all fifty-plus tables.

75
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:57,000
Check the users table —

76
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,000
you'll find the demo admin account

77
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
with email demo_admin@example.com.

78
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,000
The deals table has sample deals in various stages

79
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:13,000
from prospecting to closed-won.

80
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,000
This confirms both migrations and seeders ran correctly.

81
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
Start the development server.

82
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Run php artisan serve.

83
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Laravel's built-in server starts on port 8000 by default.

84
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,000
You'll see the message

85
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Server running on http://127.0.0.1:8000.

86
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Keep this terminal window open —

87
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:45,000
the server runs as long as this process is active.

88
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:49,000
To use a different port, add --port=8080.

89
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:53,000
To allow access from other devices on your network,

90
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,000
add --host=0.0.0.0.

91
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Open your browser and navigate to localhost:8000.

92
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:05,000
You should see the VentureX login page loading.

93
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
The initial load takes a moment

94
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:13,000
as Vite development assets compile on first request —

95
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,000
this is normal.

96
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:21,000
Once loaded, you'll see the login form

97
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:25,000
with the VentureX branding, email and password fields,

98
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:29,000
and the login button.

99
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:33,000
The interface uses Tailwind CSS 4

100
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
with our custom design system.

101
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:41,000
You'll notice the skeleton loading states —

102
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:45,000
those shimmer placeholders that appear while content loads.

103
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:49,000
These provide instant visual feedback

104
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:53,000
and make the app feel faster.

105
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Let's cover the most common installation errors.

106
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:01,000
First, composer install fails with a memory error —

107
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:05,000
fix this by setting memory_limit = 512M

108
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:09,000
in your php.ini file.

109
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,000
Second, npm install fails with a Node version error —

110
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,000
VentureX requires Node 20 or later.

111
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,000
Check your version with node -v

112
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:25,000
and update if needed using nvm.

113
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:29,000
Third, port 8000 is already in use —

114
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,000
either stop the other process

115
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:37,000
or use php artisan serve --port=8080.

116
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:41,000
Fourth, database connection refused —

117
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:45,000
make sure MySQL is running

118
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:49,000
and your .env credentials match your MySQL setup.

119
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:53,000
Fifth, migration fails with a table already exists error —

120
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:57,000
run php artisan migrate:fresh --seed to reset.

121
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:01,000
Before we end, let's appreciate the UI foundation.

122
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:05,000
VentureX uses a component-based interface

123
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:09,000
built with Livewire and Tailwind CSS 4.

124
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:13,000
The login page itself uses our card component,

125
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:17,000
form input components with validation states,

126
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:21,000
and a primary button component with loading animations.

127
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:25,000
Every module in the application follows

128
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:29,000
this same component library —

129
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:33,000
tables, modals, dropdowns, toast notifications, and stat cards.

130
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:37,000
In the next video, we'll log in for the first time

131
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:41,000
and take a full tour of the dashboard.

132
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,000
VentureX is installed and running.

133
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:49,000
In the next video, we'll log in with the demo credentials

134
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,000
and explore every corner of the dashboard.

135
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:57,000
I'll see you there.
